  "question_text": "What are Jean Piaget's theories of cognitive development?",
  "generated_answer": "Jean Piaget’s theory of cognitive development proposes that children progress through distinct, sequential stages that reflect qualitative changes in thinking. The stages are the preoperational (≈2–7 years), concrete operational (≈7–11 years), and formal operational (≈11 years to adulthood) phases, each characterized by new abilities such as egocentrism, conservation, reversibility, and abstract reasoning. Piaget also described how children build and adjust mental models—schemata—through assimilation (adding new information to existing schemata) and accommodation (modifying schemata to fit new information). Later research has suggested that development may be more continuous, that children reach milestones earlier than Piaget predicted, and that adults may enter a postformal stage that integrates emotion and context into reasoning.",
